General elections were held in [[Guinea-Bissau]] on 13 April 2014, with a second round for the presidential elections held on 18 May since no candidate received a majority in the first round. Several logistic problems and delays caused the elections to be repeatedly postponed, having initially been scheduled for 24 November 2013 and then 16 March 2014.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bissau-election-idUSBRE9AE0Q420131115 Guinea-Bissau postpones post-coup election until March] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201103150921/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bissau-election-idUSBRE9AE0Q420131115 |date=2020-11-03 }} Reuters, 15 November 2013</ref> In the second round, [[José Mário Vaz]] of the [[African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde]] was declared the president-elect with 62% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|title=Vaz elected President of Guinea-Bissau|url=https://www.voanews.com/a/vaz-elected-president-of-guinea-bissau-/1918556.html|newspaper=Voice Of America|date=20 May 2014|access-date=20 May 2014|archive-date=21 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140521031343/http://www.voanews.com/content/vaz-elected-president-of-guinea-bissau-/1918556.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Background==
{{main|2012 Guinea-Bissau coup d'etat}}

The elections were the result of a [[2012 Guinea-Bissau coup d'état|military coup]] in 2012 cancelling the [[2012 Guinea-Bissau presidential election|elections that year]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mherrera.org/elections.htm |title=Electoral Calendar - international elections world elections |access-date=2011-05-12}}</ref> 
On 26 February 2014, the [[UN Security Council]] urged Guinea-Bissau's transitional government to abide by announced election plans, warning of sanctions against those opposing a return to constitutional order.

Former President [[Kumba Ialá]] died a few weeks before the elections.<ref>[http://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/2014/04/04/ex-president-guinea-bissau-dies Ex-president of Guinea-Bissau dies] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140405171712/http://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/2014/04/04/ex-president-guinea-bissau-dies |date=2014-04-05 }} SBS, 4 April 2014</ref>

==Electoral system==
The President were elected using the [[two-round system]], whilst the 102 members of the [[National People's Assembly (Guinea-Bissau)|National People's Assembly]] were elected using [[proportional representation]] from 27 multi-member constituencies.<ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2133_B.htm Electoral system] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140414224310/http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2133_B.htm |date=2014-04-14 }} IPU</ref> Article 33 of Guinea-Bissau's Electoral Law prohibits the publishing of any opinion polls.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.eueom.eu/files/pressreleases/english/preliminarystatementgb14.04.2014enfinal.pdf|title = Holding of Elections Brings Guinea Bissau Closer to Constitutional Normalcy|date = 14 April 2014|location = Hotel Azalai, Bissau|website = European Union Election Observation Mission|access-date = 5 May 2014|archive-date = 5 May 2014|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140505234652/http://www.eueom.eu/files/pressreleases/english/preliminarystatementgb14.04.2014enfinal.pdf|url-status = live}}</ref>

==Candidates and parties==
Thirteen presidential candidates were confirmed by the High Court of Justice,<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://allafrica.com/stories/201403270247.html?|title = Guinea Bissau: Tight Timeline As Nation Prepares April 13 Polls|date = 26 March 2014|last = Look|first = Anne|access-date = 14 April 2014|website = allAfrica|archive-date = 15 April 2014|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140415040337/http://allafrica.com/stories/201403270247.html|url-status = live}}</ref> whilst eight candidates were rejected.<ref name=AN>[https://web.archive.org/web/20140421082324/http://www.anacao.cv/online/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=4420:guine-bissau-supremo-chumba-oito-candidaturas-presidenciais-e-sete-partidos&catid=87:destaque&Itemid=475 GUINÉ-BISSAU: SUPREMO “CHUMBA” OITO CANDIDATURAS PRESIDENCIAIS E SETE PARTIDOS] A Nação, 16 March 2014</ref>

The Court approved fifteen parties to contest the National People's Assembly election, but rejected applications from seven other parties; the National African Congress, the [[Guinean Civic Forum-Social Democracy]], the Democratic Party for Development, the [[Guinean Democratic Movement]], the Patriotic Movement, the [[Guinean League for Ecological Protection]] and the Party for Democracy, Development and Citizenship.<ref name=AN/>

